  "prediction": "The report outlines the methodology and key elements of an economic analysis conducted by the Government Accountability Office (GAO). An economic analysis is defined as an assessment of the economic effects of a government action, including costs, benefits, and transfers. GAO identified five key elements to a well-structured economic analysis: objective and scope, methodology, analysis of effects, transparency, and documentation.\n\nThe objective and scope of the analysis explains the action being examined and sets the boundaries for the analysis. The methodology used to examine the economic effects includes comparing alternatives and justifying the assumptions and data used. The analysis of effects quantifies and monetizes important economic effects, applying the net present value criterion to compare alternatives. Transparency requires describing and justifying analytical choices, assessing the implications of key limitations, and quantifying the statistical variability of key data elements. Documentation includes clear writing, labeling tables, and citing all sources used.\n\nGAO's assessment methodology evaluates each key element and makes an overall assessment of the economic analysis's ability to inform decision-makers and stakeholders about the economic effects of the action examined. The assessment is based on expertise in economics and professional judgment, with guiding principles of objectivity, integrity, and compliance with generally accepted government auditing standards.\n\nThe report also mentions various documents and resources used in the development of GAO's assessment methodology, including OMB Circulars, agency handbooks, and international best practices. The work was conducted in accordance with GAO's Quality Assurance Framework and underwent external review by experts.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the methodology and key elements of an economic analysis conducted by the Government Accountability Office (GAO). An economic analysis is defined as an assessment of the economic effects of a government action, including costs, benefits, and transfers. GAO identified five key elements to a well-structured economic analysis: objective and scope, methodology, analysis of effects, transparency, and documentation.\n\nThe objective and scope of the analysis explains the action being examined and sets the boundaries for the analysis. The methodology used to examine the economic effects includes comparing alternatives and justifying the assumptions and data used. The analysis of effects quantifies and monetizes important economic effects, applying the net present value criterion to compare alternatives. Transparency requires describing and justifying analytical choices, assessing the implications of key limitations, and quantifying the statistical variability of key data elements. Documentation includes clear writing, labeling tables, and citing all sources used.\n\nGAO's assessment methodology evaluates each key element and makes an overall assessment of the economic analysis's ability to inform decision-makers and stakeholders about the economic effects of the action examined. The assessment is based on expertise in economics and professional judgment, with guiding principles of objectivity, integrity, and compliance with generally accepted government auditing standards.\n\nThe report also mentions various documents and resources used in the development of GAO's assessment methodology, including OMB Circulars, agency handbooks, and international best practices. The work was conducted in accordance with GAO's Quality Assurance Framework and underwent external review by experts.",
  "score": 29.946523682576,
  "answers": [
    "We prepared this report to answer the question: What are key methodological elements of an economic analysis that is intended to inform decision-makers and stakeholders? GAO identifies five key methodological elements to the baseline structure of an economic analysis: Objective and scope, Methodology, Analysis of effects, Transparency and Documentation. GAO's assessment methodology evaluates each key element and provides an overall assessment based on the assessment of the individual key elements."
